   "answer": "\u2192 **The setup:** When you buy or sell options, big market makers take the other side and have to buy or sell the real stock to stay neutral so they don't get blown up.\n\n\u2192 **Gamma's job:** Gamma measures how fast the dealer's required stock hedge changes when the underlying price moves. \n\n\u2192 **Positive GEX (sticky):** If dealers are net long options, they have to buy dips and sell rips to stay neutral, acting like speed bumps that pin the market in a tight range.\n\n\u2192 **Negative GEX (gasoline):** If dealers are net short options, they have to chase the price\u2014selling more as it drops or buying as it flies\u2014turning a normal move into a violent vacuum."
